$ grep 'Retention sweep' /tmp/audit-final-server.log
{"level":"debug","msg":"Retention sweep complete","ts":"2026-09-03T23:11:06.460Z","purged":324,"intentsPruned":0,"sessionsPruned":1}
{"level":"debug","msg":"Retention sweep complete","ts":"2026-09-03T23:26:06.449Z","purged":10,"intentsPruned":0,"sessionsPruned":0}
{"level":"debug","msg":"Retention sweep complete","ts":"2026-09-03T23:41:06.444Z","purged":0,"intentsPruned":0,"sessionsPruned":0}
{"level":"debug","msg":"Retention sweep complete","ts":"2026-09-03T23:56:06.440Z","purged":0,"intentsPruned":0,"sessionsPruned":0}

Two facts are readable from those four lines and neither comes from a unit test:

1. `sessionsPruned: 1` — the recurring sweep DELETED a genuinely expired session row
   in a deployed standalone artifact. The field did not exist before 371f4ef.
2. The sweep re-schedules itself: 23:11:06 → 23:26:06 → 23:41:06 → 23:56:06, four
   consecutive firings exactly 900 s apart, with no request touching it. Every unit
   test in this repository builds the handler itself, so all of them stay green in a
   deployment where the lazy `ensureWorkerReady()` never fires and nothing expires.

$ sqlite3 /tmp/audit-final-db.db \
    "select count(*), sum(case when expiresAt < strftime('%s','now')*1000 then 1 else 0 end) from sessions;"
sessions total: 27  ·  still expired and stored: 0
expiresAt sqlite type: integer

The sqlite type is checked because a millisecond epoch stored as TEXT compares
lexicographically and the prune would silently match nothing.

Purge counts fall to zero and stay there (324 → 10 → 0 → 0) rather than repeating a
number, which is what a sweep that deletes rather than merely selects looks like.
